[11:09:36] [INFO] STEP 5.54: EXACT PROOF OF TEP SCALING IDENTITY
[11:09:36] [INFO] ================================================================================
[11:09:36] [INFO] 
[11:09:36] [INFO]     MATHEMATICAL PROOF: Γ_TEP = 2Γ_N - 1 is Exact for OLS Slopes
[11:09:36] [INFO]     ----------------------------------------------------------
[11:09:36] [INFO]     Let X = log(ρ_c)
[11:09:36] [INFO]     Let R = log(R_c)
[11:09:36] [INFO] 
[11:09:36] [INFO]     The observables scale as:
[11:09:36] [INFO]     Newtonian acceleration: a ∝ ρ_c * R_c  =>  log(a) = X + R + C_1
[11:09:36] [INFO]     TEP potential:         |Φ| ∝ ρ_c * R_c^2 => log(|Φ|) = X + 2R + C_2
[11:09:36] [INFO] 
[11:09:36] [INFO]     The OLS slope (Γ) of any variable Y against X is defined by:
[11:09:36] [INFO]     Γ = Cov(Y, X) / Var(X)
[11:09:36] [INFO] 
[11:09:36] [INFO]     For the Newtonian slope:
[11:09:36] [INFO]     Γ_N = Cov(X + R, X) / Var(X)
[11:09:36] [INFO]         = [Var(X) + Cov(R, X)] / Var(X)
[11:09:36] [INFO]         = 1 + Cov(R, X)/Var(X)
[11:09:36] [INFO] 
[11:09:36] [INFO]     Let α_eff = Cov(R, X)/Var(X). Note that α_eff is precisely the OLS slope
[11:09:36] [INFO]     of R vs X. This does NOT require R to be a deterministic function of X;
[11:09:36] [INFO]     it is just the definition of the regression slope, fully valid for data
[11:09:36] [INFO]     with arbitrary scatter.
[11:09:36] [INFO] 
[11:09:36] [INFO]     Thus, Γ_N = 1 + α_eff
[11:09:36] [INFO] 
[11:09:36] [INFO]     For the TEP slope:
[11:09:36] [INFO]     Γ_TEP = Cov(X + 2R, X) / Var(X)
[11:09:36] [INFO]           = [Var(X) + 2Cov(R, X)] / Var(X)
[11:09:36] [INFO]           = 1 + 2[Cov(R, X)/Var(X)]
[11:09:36] [INFO]           = 1 + 2α_eff
[11:09:36] [INFO] 
[11:09:36] [INFO]     Substituting α_eff = Γ_N - 1 into the TEP equation:
[11:09:36] [INFO]     Γ_TEP = 1 + 2(Γ_N - 1)
[11:09:36] [INFO]     Γ_TEP = 2Γ_N - 1
[11:09:36] [INFO] 
[11:09:36] [INFO]     CONCLUSION:
[11:09:36] [INFO]     This identity is EXACT for regression slopes. It does not assume α is
[11:09:36] [INFO]     constant, nor does it assume zero scatter in the R_c vs ρ_c relationship.
[11:09:36] [INFO]     It relies purely on the linearity of covariance.
[11:09:36] [INFO] 
[11:09:36] [INFO] --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[11:09:36] [INFO] EMPIRICAL MONTE CARLO DEMONSTRATION
